kosha electrical-varga

  -- electrical engineering: power systems at scale.
  -- the discipline of generating, transmitting, and converting electrical energy.
  -- distinguishing from electronics: electrical = power (watts to megawatts).
  --                                  electronics = signal (milliwatts, information).
  --
  -- the power chain:
  --   generation:    turbine (steam, water, wind) → generator → AC voltage.
  --                  generator: mechanical rotation → electromagnetic induction → EMF.
  --                  Faraday: EMF = -dΦ/dt. rotating coil in magnetic field.
  --   transmission:  step-up transformer → high voltage (11kV, 132kV, 400kV).
  --                  high voltage → low current → P=IV → low I²R losses in cables.
  --   distribution:  step-down transformers → 11kV → 415V (3-phase) → 230V (single phase).
  --   consumption:   motors, heaters, lighting, electronics.
  --
  -- three-phase power: three AC voltages 120° apart.
  --   used for all large-scale transmission and industrial motors.
  --   advantages: constant total power (no pulsation), smaller conductors.
  --   star connection: neutral wire. delta connection: no neutral.
  --
  -- transformer: the device that makes AC power distribution possible.
  --   V1/V2 = N1/N2 (turns ratio). P1 = P2 (ideal, no losses).
  --   works ONLY with AC — changing flux induces EMF.
  --   DC through a transformer: constant flux → no EMF → short circuit.
  --
  -- power factor: cos φ. ratio of real power to apparent power.
  --   unity power factor (pf=1): purely resistive load. all energy useful.
  --   inductive load (motors, transformers): pf < 1. current lags voltage.
  --   capacitive correction: capacitors cancel inductance → pf → 1.
  --
  -- protection:
  --   fuse: melts at overcurrent. one-time use.
  --   circuit breaker: trips and resets. magnetic or thermal-magnetic.
  --   earth leakage (RCD/GFCI): detects current imbalance → shock protection.
  --   lightning arrester: diverts surge to earth.

  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

  -- physics connections
  "electromagnetism-yukta"
